module

public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.AmpleEventualGeneration.Basic
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.SectionProduct.Basic
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.NonvanishingSpelling.Basic
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Cohomology.GlobalGeneration.Stalks

/-!
# Generation by finitely many nonvanishing global sections

Let `M` be an invertible `𝒪_X`-module on a scheme and let `s_1, …, s_n` be global sections of `M`
whose nonvanishing loci cover `X`. Then `M` is generated by those `n` sections. This is the
instance of [Stacks, Tag 01AN] that the proof of clause (7) of [Stacks, Tag 01Q3] consumes:
the source says that `L ^⊗ d` is globally generated by `s_1^{d/d_1}, …, s_n^{d/d_n}` and does not
argue the point, because for an invertible module "does not vanish at `x`" *is* "generates the
stalk at `x`".

Both inputs are proved elsewhere:

* `Stacks.SectionProduct.exists_smul_Γgerm` — Nakayama at a rank-one stalk: the germ of a global
  section not vanishing at `x` generates `M_x` over `𝒪_{X,x}`;
* `AlgebraicGeometry.generates_iff_stalkwise_generates` — a family of global sections generates a
  sheaf of modules exactly when its germs span every stalk.

The gap between them is that the two speak of **different stalk rings**, and that is the content of
the first half of this file.

## The scalar-spelling gap, and what it is not

`AlgebraicGeometry.generates_iff_stalkwise_generates` takes its span over
`(TopCat.Presheaf.stalk O_X.obj x : RingCat)`, the stalk of the `RingCat`-valued structure sheaf,
with Mathlib's `RingCat` module instance
`PresheafOfModules.instModuleCarrierStalkRingCatCarrierAbPresheafOpensCarrier`.
`Stacks.SectionProduct.exists_smul_Γgerm` produces a scalar in `X.presheaf.stalk x`, the stalk of
the `CommRingCat`-valued structure presheaf, acting through Mathlib's `CommRingCat` instance
`PresheafOfModules.instModuleCarrierStalkCommRingCatCarrierAbPresheafOpensCarrier`
(which `Stacks.ModuleStalk.stalkModule` names).

**The two stalk rings are not the same type**, and this is not the hazard that
`Stacks.NonvanishingPullback.stalkModule_eq` bridges. The
carriers `↥(TopCat.Presheaf.stalk X.ringCatSheaf.obj x)` and `↥(X.presheaf.stalk x)` are not
`rfl`-equal — they are filtered colimits taken in `RingCat` and in `CommRingCat`, and the failure
message names `CategoryTheory.Limits.getColimitCocone` as a definition it declines to unfold. So the
existing bridge does **not** serve: it equates two module structures over *one* ring,
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.TensorStalkRingedSpace.stalkModule` against Mathlib's, whereas here the two
  module structures are over
*two different rings*.

**No ring homomorphism between the two stalks is built, and none is needed.** All that a span
argument requires is that the `𝒪_{X,x}`-orbit of a stalk element be inside its orbit under the
`RingCat` stalk, and that is settled one element at a time:
`Stacks.FinGenFromCover.exists_ringStalk_smul_eq` says that for every `r : X.presheaf.stalk x` and
every `m` there is an `r'` in the `RingCat` stalk with `r • m = r' • m`. Its proof is three lines
of germ bookkeeping — represent `r` and `m` on a common open, and read `r₀ • m₀` through
`Stacks.ModuleStalk.germ_smul` one way and `PresheafOfModules.germ_ringCat_smul` the other, both
of which characterise their action by the *same* formula on the *same* section `r₀ • m₀`.
Constructing the comparison ring homomorphism would mean transporting a filtered colimit across
`forget₂ CommRingCat RingCat`, which is strictly more work for strictly less use.

## Main definitions

* `Stacks.FinGenFromCover.ringStalkModule`: the `RingCat`-stalk module structure on a module
  stalk, named so that it is found through the `Scheme.Modules` wrapper. A `scoped instance`, for
  the reason `Stacks.ModuleStalk.stalkModule` gives for its own.

## Main results

* `Stacks.FinGenFromCover.ringStalk_germ_smul`: germs respect scalars for the `RingCat` stalk.
* `Stacks.FinGenFromCover.exists_ringStalk_smul_eq`: **the scalar bridge.** Every `𝒪_{X,x}`-scalar
  multiple of a stalk element is a `RingCat`-stalk scalar multiple of it.
* `Stacks.FinGenFromCover.span_eq_top_of_mem_nonvanishingLocus`: for an invertible `M`, if some
  member of a family of global sections does not vanish at `x` then the germs of the family span
  the stalk at `x`.
* `Stacks.FinGenFromCover.isFinGen_of_nonvanishingCover`: **the target.** An invertible
  `𝒪_X`-module with a finite family of global sections whose nonvanishing loci cover `X` is
  generated by finitely many global sections, in the sense of
  `Stacks.AmpleEventualGeneration.IsFinGen`.

## Implementation notes

**Global sections against `M.sections`.** `Stacks.AmpleEventualGeneration.IsFinGen` is phrased
through Mathlib's `SheafOfModules.GeneratingSections`, whose `s` field is a family of
restriction-compatible families `M.sections`, while `Stacks.Ample.nonvanishingLocus` takes a bare
`Γ(M, ⊤)`. `Stacks.NonvanishingSpelling.sectionsEquiv` is the bijection, and
`Stacks.NonvanishingSpelling.sectionsEquiv_apply` is `rfl`, so passing from one to the other costs
a single `Equiv.apply_symm_apply` inside the proof and nothing in the statement.

**`M.presheaf` versus `M.val.presheaf`.** Instance search finds Mathlib's `RingCat` stalk-module
instance for `TopCat.Presheaf.stalk M.val.presheaf x` and *not* for
`TopCat.Presheaf.stalk (C := Ab) M.presheaf x`, although `Scheme.Modules.presheaf M` is
`M.val.presheaf` by definition: the two are a `def` apart and instance search is syntactic. This
is "Transparency gotcha 1" of `StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/AmplePowers/ModuleStalk.lean` at the
other stalk ring, and `Stacks.FinGenFromCover.ringStalkModule` closes it the same way, by naming the
instance with its arguments written out. Consequently the span in
`Stacks.FinGenFromCover.span_eq_top_of_mem_nonvanishingLocus` is stated over the *named* instance
while `AlgebraicGeometry.generates_iff_stalkwise_generates` states its own over the anonymous one;
the two are one delta-reduction apart, which `exact` performs and `infer_instance` would not — so
the application in `isFinGen_of_nonvanishingCover` is by `exact`, deliberately.

**The covering hypothesis is pointwise, not a union.** `∀ x, ∃ i, x ∈ X_{s i}` rather than
`⋃ i, X_{s i} = ⊤`, because the stalkwise criterion is applied one point at a time and a
consumer extracting a finite subcover from quasi-compactness holds exactly this shape.

No tag attribute is attached. [Stacks, Tag 01AN] is the equivalence, for an arbitrary ringed space
and an arbitrary index set, between generation and generation of every stalk; that equivalence is
`AlgebraicGeometry.generates_iff_stalkwise_generates`, which is in the library and is not this file. What
is proved here is one direction of one corollary of it, for a finite family of sections of an
invertible module on a scheme, so attaching the label would overclaim it. Tag 01Q3 asserts the
equivalence of all nine of its clauses, and half of one clause earns no part of that label either.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 01AN](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01AN)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 01CY](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01CY)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 01Q3](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01Q3)

## What is not done

**The comparison ring homomorphism `X.presheaf.stalk x ⟶ (X.ringCatSheaf.obj).stalk x` is not
built**, and neither is the isomorphism it would be. Nothing here identifies the two stalk rings;
only the two scalar *actions on a fixed element* are reconciled. A consumer that needs to compare
`Submodule.span` over the one ring with `Submodule.span` over the other, in both directions, or
that needs `IsLocalRing` of the `RingCat` stalk, will have to build that iso — the route is
`CategoryTheory.Limits.preservesColimitIso (forget₂ CommRingCat RingCat)` applied to
`(OpenNhds.inclusion x).op ⋙ X.presheaf`, together with `ι_preservesColimitIso_hom` to identify
its germs.

**Only one direction is proved.** That a family of sections generating `M` must have, at each
point, a member not vanishing there is false for `|I| > 1` and is not claimed; the true converse
is the span statement, which is `AlgebraicGeometry.generates_iff_stalkwise_generates` itself.

**Invertibility is used essentially.** `Stacks.SectionProduct.exists_smul_Γgerm` is Nakayama at a
*rank-one* stalk; for a module of higher rank a single nonvanishing section never generates, and
the finite-cover statement has no analogue in that generality.
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

open AlgebraicGeometry CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits TopologicalSpace Opposite
open Stacks.Ample AlgebraicGeometry.SheafOfModules
open scoped Stacks.ModuleStalk

universe u

namespace Stacks.FinGenFromCover

variable {X : Scheme.{u}}

/-! ### The module structure over the `RingCat` stalk -/

/-- The module structure on the stalk of an `𝒪_X`-module over the stalk of the **`RingCat`-valued**
structure sheaf `X.ringCatSheaf`. This is Mathlib's instance for a presheaf of modules over a
presheaf of rings, applied with the base presheaf written as `X.ringCatSheaf.obj` and the module
as `M.val`; instance search does not find it through the `Scheme.Modules` wrapper, for the reason
`Stacks.ModuleStalk.stalkModule` records at the `CommRingCat` stalk.

This is the ring the span of
`AlgebraicGeometry.generates_iff_stalkwise_generates` is taken over, and it is **not** the ring
`Stacks.ModuleStalk.stalkModule` acts by; see the module documentation. -/
noncomputable scoped instance ringStalkModule (M : X.Modules) (x : X) :
    Module (TopCat.Presheaf.stalk X.ringCatSheaf.obj x : RingCat)
      (TopCat.Presheaf.stalk (C := Ab.{u}) M.presheaf x) :=
  PresheafOfModules.instModuleCarrierStalkRingCatCarrierAbPresheafOpensCarrier
    (R := X.ringCatSheaf.obj) M.val x

/-- The germ of a scalar multiple is the scalar multiple of the germs, with the scalar read in the
`RingCat` stalk. -/
theorem ringStalk_germ_smul (M : X.Modules) (x : X) (U : X.Opens) (hx : x ∈ U)
    (r : Γ(X, U)) (t : Γ(M, U)) :
    (M.presheaf.germ U x hx).hom (r • t) =
      (TopCat.Presheaf.germ X.ringCatSheaf.obj U x hx).hom r •
        (M.presheaf.germ U x hx).hom t :=
  PresheafOfModules.germ_ringCat_smul (R := X.ringCatSheaf.obj) M.val x U hx r t

/-- **The scalar bridge.** An `𝒪_{X,x}`-scalar multiple of an element of a module stalk is also a
scalar multiple of it over the stalk of the `RingCat`-valued structure sheaf.

Both actions are characterised by the same formula on germs, so representing the scalar and the
stalk element on a common open reduces the claim to the identity `r₀ • m₀ = r₀ • m₀` in
`Γ(M, U ⊓ V)`. No ring homomorphism between the two stalks is constructed. -/
theorem exists_ringStalk_smul_eq (M : X.Modules) (x : X)
    (r : X.presheaf.stalk x) (m : TopCat.Presheaf.stalk (C := Ab.{u}) M.presheaf x) :
    ∃ r' : (TopCat.Presheaf.stalk X.ringCatSheaf.obj x : RingCat), r • m = r' • m := by
  obtain ⟨U, hU, r₀, hr⟩ := TopCat.Presheaf.exists_germ_eq X.presheaf r
  obtain ⟨V, hV, m₀, hm⟩ := TopCat.Presheaf.exists_germ_eq (C := Ab.{u}) M.presheaf m
  have hxW : x ∈ U ⊓ V := ⟨hU, hV⟩
  set r₁ := X.presheaf.map (homOfLE (inf_le_left (a := U) (b := V))).op r₀ with hr₁
  set m₁ := M.presheaf.map (homOfLE (inf_le_right (a := U) (b := V))).op m₀ with hm₁
  have hrW : (X.presheaf.germ (U ⊓ V) x hxW).hom r₁ = r :=
    (TopCat.Presheaf.germ_res_apply X.presheaf (homOfLE inf_le_left) x hxW r₀).trans hr
  have hmW : (TopCat.Presheaf.germ (C := Ab.{u}) M.presheaf (U ⊓ V) x hxW).hom m₁ = m :=
    (TopCat.Presheaf.germ_res_apply (C := Ab.{u}) M.presheaf (homOfLE inf_le_right) x hxW
      m₀).trans hm
  refine ⟨(TopCat.Presheaf.germ X.ringCatSheaf.obj (U ⊓ V) x hxW).hom r₁, ?_⟩
  rw [← hrW, ← hmW, ← Stacks.ModuleStalk.germ_smul, ringStalk_germ_smul]

/-! ### Nonvanishing at a point is spanning of the stalk there -/

/-- **A family of global sections of an invertible module spans the stalk at every point at which
one of them does not vanish.** This is Nakayama at a rank-one stalk
(`Stacks.SectionProduct.exists_smul_Γgerm`) stated over the stalk ring that
`AlgebraicGeometry.generates_iff_stalkwise_generates` uses, through
`Stacks.FinGenFromCover.exists_ringStalk_smul_eq`. -/
theorem span_eq_top_of_mem_nonvanishingLocus (M : X.Modules) [IsInvertibleModule X M] {I : Type u}
    (t : I → Γ(M, ⊤)) {x : X} {i : I} (hx : x ∈ nonvanishingLocus M (t i)) :
    Submodule.span (R := (TopCat.Presheaf.stalk X.ringCatSheaf.obj x : RingCat))
      (Set.range fun j => (M.presheaf.Γgerm x).hom (t j)) = ⊤ := by
  refine eq_top_iff.mpr fun w _ => ?_
  obtain ⟨r, hr⟩ := Stacks.SectionProduct.exists_smul_Γgerm M (t i) hx w
  obtain ⟨r', hr'⟩ := exists_ringStalk_smul_eq M x r ((M.presheaf.Γgerm x).hom (t i))
  rw [hr, hr']
  exact Submodule.smul_mem _ r' (Submodule.subset_span ⟨i, rfl⟩)

/-- **An invertible `𝒪_X`-module whose nonvanishing loci of finitely many global sections cover
`X` is generated by those sections.**

This is the instance of [Stacks, Tag 01AN] that the source's proof of clause (7) of
[Stacks, Tag 01Q3] uses without comment when it asserts that `L ^⊗ d` is globally generated by
`s_1^{d/d_1}, …, s_n^{d/d_n}`. The index type is only required to be `Finite`, which is exactly
what `Stacks.AmpleEventualGeneration.IsFinGen` asks of a generating family. -/
theorem isFinGen_of_nonvanishingCover (M : X.Modules) [IsInvertibleModule X M] {I : Type u}
    [Finite I] (t : I → Γ(M, ⊤)) (hcov : ∀ x : X, ∃ i, x ∈ nonvanishingLocus M (t i)) :
    Stacks.AmpleEventualGeneration.IsFinGen M := by
  refine ⟨⟨I, fun i => (Stacks.NonvanishingSpelling.sectionsEquiv X M).symm (t i), ?_⟩, ‹_›⟩
  refine (AlgebraicGeometry.generates_iff_stalkwise_generates X.ringCatSheaf M _).mpr fun x => ?_
  obtain ⟨i, hi⟩ := hcov x
  have hval : ∀ j : I,
      ((Stacks.NonvanishingSpelling.sectionsEquiv X M).symm (t j)).eval (op ⊤) = t j :=
    fun j => (Stacks.NonvanishingSpelling.sectionsEquiv X M).apply_symm_apply (t j)
  simp only [hval]
  exact span_eq_top_of_mem_nonvanishingLocus M t hi

end Stacks.FinGenFromCover

end
